2026-09-24 — kselftest harness source-order expectation.

Frozen harness: KSELFTEST_PRIO_TEST_F=20000, default-priority direction
detector, __LIST_APPEND prepends while the detector is false. The three
fixture registrations therefore run before detection, producing 321 rather
than the expected 123. This is an upstream harness assumption, not a kernel
or EvoCC constructor-order defect.

The small C reduction records both registration trace and resulting list.
GCC-14 / Clang-19 / EvoCC at O0/O2 all reproduce the source-order assertion
failure (exit 1), accept the observed order (exit 0), and pass a causal
control moving only the detector to priority 10000 (exit 0). Eighteen native
observations, including six expected REDs; zero compilation errors. Six
C-HIR/EvoIR executions give the same outcomes. No production ordering rule,
upstream source, expected output or timeout is changed by the control.

The unchanged original harness-selftest.sh is replayed on the existing exact
GCC/EvoCC Images and test disks. BOTH remain FAIL (exit 1): the only output
diff is fixture pass/timeout ordering, with fail remaining in the middle.
Each test's behavior is otherwise the expected behavior. Both guests boot
and power off normally, and snapshot-backed disk hashes remain unchanged.
The 45-second wrapper remains. This closes K11b's causal classification,
not the original as PASS, K11's population, or a new complete campaign.

Changed gate against c83f3b3: 506 PASS / 0 FAIL / 0 ignored, 44 groups;
Clippy -D warnings, fmt and diff checks PASS. CLI regression lives in the
existing elf_lifecycles test. No compiler implementation change was needed.

